          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Duke of Edinburgh's Role</strong><br>\nAs consort to Queen Elizabeth II, Philip had no constitutional role, but he took on many public duties. He accompanied the Queen on state visits and tours and represented the monarchy at public events. He was known for his strong character and dedication to public service, often supporting the Queen in her duties.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Public Life</strong><br>\nPrince Philip was involved in numerous charitable endeavors and was particularly interested in scientific and technological research, conservation, and the environment. He founded the Duke of Edinburgh's Award in 1956, which has since encouraged millions of young people to engage in self-improvement and community service.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Controversial Remarks</strong><br>\nThroughout his public life, Philip was known for his sharp wit and sometimes controversial remarks. He often made headlines for his off-the-cuff comments, which were sometimes perceived as gaffes. However, his forthrightness and sense of humor were also considered by many to be part of his charm.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
